<DetailLayout
  title="Tibetan Toast Feast and Bonfire Night"
  description="A Tibetan toast feast in Shangri-La: yak meat platters, warm barley wine, live song and dance, and a bonfire circle under the stars. How to book."
  category="Food"
  image="tibetan-barley-wine.webp"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "Tibetan Toast Feast: Shangri-La's Dinner and Bonfire Night",
    "description": "Guide to the Tibetan chieftain-style toast feast in Shangri-La Yunnan: endless yak meat platters, warm barley wine, live folk performance, and bonfire circle dancing with locals. Prices, venues, and booking tips.",
    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Shangri-La Guide"}
  }}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Tibetan Toast Feast &amp; Bonfire</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-500">A traditional Tibetan dinner served in a historic chieftain's house (土司宴). Endless platters of yak meat flow alongside warm highland barley wine, live Tibetan singing and dancing, and the night ends outside in a bonfire circle &mdash; locals and travelers dancing together under the stars. For most visitors it is the single most memorable evening in Shangri-La.</p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What the Feast Is</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The toast feast recreates the banquet style of the old Tibetan chieftains (tusi) who ruled the Shangri-La region before 1950. Guests sit low at long wooden tables in a carved Tibetan hall. Dishes arrive in waves: <strong>hand-cut yak meat, yak sausage, roasted barley (tsampa), potato dishes, and sweet tea</strong>, washed down with <a href="/food/tibetan-barley-wine/" class="text-emerald-700 underline underline-offset-2">warm highland barley wine</a> poured from a small pot.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Between courses, performers in full traditional dress sing work songs, play yak-mimicking dances, and invite guests to join a <strong>khatag scarf presentation</strong> &mdash; the white silk blessing scarf offering. The house host explains each dish and toast custom as it comes, in halting but enthusiastic English at the tourist-facing venues.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">How the Evening Runs</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Opening &mdash; Seating and Welcome Toast</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Seatings begin in the early evening; arrive on time. The banquet opens with a collective toast, the host's greeting song, and a <strong>khatag scarf presentation</strong> &mdash; the white silk blessing offering. Latecomers miss it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Middle &mdash; Courses and Performance</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Dishes arrive in waves with a song or dance set between rounds: hand-cut yak meat, yak sausage, roasted barley (tsampa), potato dishes, and sweet tea, with <a href="/food/tibetan-barley-wine/" class="text-emerald-700 underline underline-offset-2">warm barley wine</a> refilled from a small pot. Pace yourself.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Closing &mdash; Bonfire Circle Dance</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Everyone moves outside around a lit bonfire. The circle dance (guozhuang) has simple repeating steps &mdash; follow the person in front; nobody judges footwork. This is the part people film for ten minutes and remember for years.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Price and How to Book</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Budget house feasts:</strong> lower-priced set menus at family-run Tibetan houses near Dukezong &mdash; simpler food, larger tourist groups, same bonfire.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Heritage manor feasts:</strong> a premium ticket at restored chieftain manors such as the Zhaya Tusi Manor, with fuller performance casts and higher production value. Compare current prices on OTAs or at your hotel desk.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Booking:</strong> your hotel or guesthouse front desk arranges it the same day &mdash; just ask for a "tu si yan" (吐司宴) or toast feast. English-language OTA listings also carry the manor venues. Evenings run year-round, roughly 30&ndash;80 guests per seating.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Dietary note:</strong> the menu is heavily yak-meat based. Vegetarians are accommodated at some houses if mentioned when booking, but options are limited &mdash; consider ordering extra vegetable dishes.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 p-6">
